course: dictionary
lesson: 15918
title: Unpick a seam or a problem
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To unpick means to take something apart carefully, one piece at a time. In sewing, you unpick a seam by pulling out its stitches one by one. «Mia sewed the sleeve on crooked, so she had to unpick it and start again.»
explain: The word also has a clever meaning. When you unpick a problem, you look carefully at each part so you understand it. «Let's unpick this question and find what it is really asking.» Nobody is sewing there. The question is being looked at, bit by bit.
explain: Use the context to choose the meaning. If the sentence is about thread, fabric or stitches, unpick means take out the stitches. If it is about a question, a plan or an argument, unpick means take it apart to understand it or undo it. Put your meaning into the sentence and check that it makes sense.
fact: Unpick is a verb: unpick, unpicks, unpicked, unpicking.
fact: «Unpick» means to take out stitches carefully, one by one.
fact: A seam ripper is a tool that helps you unpick stitches.
fact: «Unpick» can also mean to take a problem apart carefully so you understand it.
fact: The past tense is «unpicked», as in «She unpicked the hem of her skirt».
exercise: «Sam had to unpick the stitches on his bag.» What does unpick mean here? | *Take out the stitches carefully, one by one | Wash the bag in hot water | Cut the bag into pieces with scissors | Sew a brand new bag quickly
exercise: «Let's unpick this question before we answer it.» What does unpick mean here? | Skip the question altogether | Write the answer in big letters | *Look at each part to understand it | Read it out loud very fast
exercise: Which word is the past tense of unpick? | unpick | *unpicked | unpicks | unpicking
test: «Grandma had to unpick the old jumper so she could use its wool again.» What does unpick mean here? | Throw the jumper away | *Take out the stitches carefully | Put the jumper on | Knit a jumper quickly
test: «We must unpick the plan to see why it failed.» What does unpick mean here? | Paint the plan in bright colours | Make the plan bigger | *Take the plan apart to understand it | Forget about the plan
exam: Which sentence uses unpick to mean take a problem apart to understand it? | «He sewed a button onto his coat.» | *«We unpicked the puzzle one clue at a time.» | «The rain was loud on the roof.» | «She unpicked the hem of her skirt.»
